-- title:
--   Moduli description of X_H(M) points at transcendental j
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field, $M$ a nonzero natural number with $M \neq 0$ in $K$, and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ a subgroup; write $\Gamma_H(M)$ for the subgroup [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) of $SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$ consisting of the matrices in $\Gamma_0(M)$ whose unit image under [`CohCarrier.gamma0Units M`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L121) lies in $H$, and $F =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC K (CohCarrier.GammaH M H)`, the intermediate field of $K((q))$ (Laurent series, i.e. Hahn series over $\mathbb{Z}$) generated over $K$ by the quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_f)/\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_g)$, where $p_f,p_g \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ are integral $q$-expansions at $\infty$ of two modular forms of one and the same weight on $\Gamma_H(M)$ with $\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_g) \neq 0$, the series being transported to $K$ coefficientwise. Let $x \in F$ have Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot$(the image in $K[[q]]$ of `jNum`), the $q$-expansion of $j$. Let $k \subseteq \Omega$ be fields over $K$ with $K \to k \to \Omega$ a scalar tower, and let $E$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $k$ with $j(E)$ transcendental over $K$ and such that the group of $\Omega$-points of the affine curve $E_\Omega$ killed by $M$ has exactly $M^2$ elements. Then there is a map $\Psi$ assigning to each point $P \in E_\Omega(\Omega)$ of exact additive order $M$ a $K$-algebra homomorphism $\Psi_P : F \to \Omega$ with $\Psi_P(x) = j(E)$ in $\Omega$, such that: (i) for every $\sigma \in \mathrm{Aut}_k(\Omega)$ and points $P,P'$ of order $M$ with $P' = \sigma(P)$ (coordinatewise via `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map`), $\Psi_{P'} = \sigma \circ \Psi_P$; (ii) for every homomorphism $\rho$ from $\Gamma_0(M)$ to the $K$-algebra automorphisms of $F$ satisfying `IsDiamondPullbackModL K M H ρ` — that is, whenever $f_1 = f\mid_k\gamma$ and $g_1 = g\mid_k\gamma$ for modular forms on $\Gamma_H(M)$ of weight $k$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g,p_{f_1},p_{g_1}$, $\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_g) \neq 0$, every element of $F$ with series $\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_{f_1})/\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_{g_1})$ is carried by $\rho(\gamma)$ to $\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_f)/\mathrm{intSeriesC}_K(p_g)$ — and for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M)$ and points $P,P'$ of order $M$ with $P' = a\cdot P$, where $a$ is the natural representative of the upper-left entry of $\gamma$ modulo $M$, one has $\Psi_{P'} = \Psi_P \circ \rho(\gamma)$; (iii) if $u \in H \sqcup \langle -1 \rangle$ (the subgroup generated by $H$ and $-1$) and $P' = u \cdot P$ then $\Psi_{P'} = \Psi_P$; (iv) conversely $\Psi_{P'} = \Psi_P$ forces $P' = u\cdot P$ for some such $u$; and (v) every $K$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : F \to \Omega$ with $\psi(x) = j(E)$ equals $\Psi_P$ for some $P$ of order $M$.
--
--   This is the moduli interpretation of the modular curve $X_H(M)$ over the $j$-line in the form needed here: the $\Omega$-valued places of the function field of $X_H(M)$ lying above a transcendental $j$-invariant correspond exactly to points of order $M$ on the curve taken modulo $\pm H$, compatibly with the Galois action and with the diamond operators. It feeds the computation of the degree of the map to the $j$-line (the index formula for $\Gamma_1(M) \le \Gamma \le \Gamma_0(M)$) and the construction of the orbit map on double cosets and on places of the function field.
